WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.560
Life begins with one cell, the
fertilized egg, that grows to a human

2
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.470
being with over 30 trillion cells. What
are the instructions that allow you to

3
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.600
become you? Let's zoom in to one cell
of your body. Cells have a long

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.270
molecule called DNA, which is the
genetic material that's passed along

5
00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:22.510
from parents to offspring. If you
stretched out the DNA from just one

6
00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:26.420
cell of your body, it would be two
meters in length, about as tall as you

7
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:30.900
are. Along this long strand of DNA are
specific segments that hold

8
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.890
instructions that tell your cells what
they're supposed to do. These segments

9
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.150
are called genes.

10
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.180
Now let's zoom in and see what a gene
is made of. The gene is a sequence of

11
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:48.820
four chemicals called nucleotide bases,
abbreviated as A, T, G and C. The

12
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.880
sequence of chemicals contains
information to make a functional

13
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:57.090
product, usually a protein or sometimes
a functional RNA molecule. Some of

14
00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.300
these products are like tools or
building blocks that help to build

15
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.050
structures of the cells. Others carry
out chemical reactions like an

16
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.280
industrial factory.

17
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.900
A human cell has a lot of genes, about
20,000, each producing a unique product.

18
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.820
Some are used in all cells of your body.
However, some genes are turned on only

19
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.460
in certain cells, such as just your
muscle cells or just your brain cells,

20
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.790
and turned off in others. And some are
only used in certain times, for example,

21
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.270
just when you're an embryo. Thus, genes
not only contain information on what

22
00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.050
kind of product to make, but also
instruct when and where to make it.

23
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.730
When a gene is turned on, it can also
regulate how much of the product should

24
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:42.370
be produced, like a dimmer switch. The
amazing thing about genes is that they

25
00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:47.540
are incredibly adaptable. They are not
rigid instruction manuals, but can turn

26
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:51.700
on or off depending on what you're
doing. For example, when you exercise,

27
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:56.850
different genes turn on or off,
allowing you to build more muscle. How

28
00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.480
do 20,000 genes turning on and off and
making different products give rise to

29
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:06.470
a living, thinking, breathing being as
interesting as you? That is a mystery

30
00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.800
that scientists are still trying to
understand.

